## Track A #173 — targeted reflow/keyboard review checks from historical rendered geometry

After the responsive TEST SVG corrections, [the prior-head actual Chromium log](https://github.com/reallakshman19/Grade9v3.5/actions/runs/38102532074) measured a **680px-wide Core1A equation table** inside a 320px document, yet document `scrollWidth` stayed 320px. This is not automatically a crop: the unchanged renderer intentionally wraps the equation table in a `.g9-table-scroll { overflow-x:auto }` region. However, its markup does not explicitly declare `tabindex` on that region. Browser-native focusability and keyboard horizontal scrolling must be **hands-on checked** (Tab, arrow keys, focus ring, VoiceOver/NVDA reading order) rather than called PASS from no root overflow. **Do not disable scroll or shrink mathematical text below policy to force a viewport fit.**

At **195 CSS px only**, earlier machine geometry recorded a narrow viewport/root overflow of ~6 CSS px and a figure wider than its visible card. The renderer's `.g9-diagram-scroll` *does* have `tabindex="0"`, `role="region"`, an explicit horizontal-scroll accessibility label and a minimum text size; a scrollable diagram and a truly cut-off diagram must be distinguished by testing keyboard scrolling and whether all factor labels remain reachable. This 195px artificial viewport is **not** an actual 200% browser zoom session.

Named #169 human reviewer: for both variants, check 200% true zoom at 390px device width, all four roles and each Core1A stage, and specifically record **Core1A table horizontal keyboard access** plus **Core1B/Core2B neutral SVG horizontal access**, using device/OS/browser/AT/zoom/screenshots. Record actual inaccessible content as a dated defect; no inferred acceptance. [Prior-head complete automated browser/A4 pass](https://github.com/reallakshman19/Grade9v3.5/actions/runs/38103057565) is technical-only; current more granular stage run is queued.

